From 7533c060fd0340d2768803bc324c8aabe5260495 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Montana <montana@linux.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 04:23:05 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 20/21] bngsim: read time-indexed tables at their breakpoints
 in stepTo

stepTo() drew each waiting time from the total propensity and read the
propensities again only after a reaction fired. A table function indexed
by the time changes value at its breakpoints whether or not anything
fires, so a rate that was 0 until a later breakpoint never turned on:
with a_tot at 0 the run jumped to the stopping time, and with a_tot tiny
the stale value held until the next firing.

The system now keeps the breakpoints of every table whose counter is the
time. A draw that passes the next one is dropped there, the propensities
are read again and the waiting time is drawn anew, which is exact for a
step table because the waiting time is memoryless; with a_tot at 0 the
run advances to the next breakpoint instead of the stopping time. A
linear table is read at its breakpoints too, and between them holds the
value it was last read at, as before. A breakpoint on the stopping time
is read there.

A table listed in decreasing time, which only a file can give, is read
one ulp past each breakpoint: tfun_interpolate_value() reads such a
table from the other side, so at the breakpoint itself a step still
gives the value from before it. The refresh after a firing calls the
same helper.

A linear table moves between its breakpoints too, and a propensity held
at its last reading lagged the ramp wherever little fired (bngsim GH
#1179): 100 X(p~1) leaving at a rate that ramps from 0 at t = 0 stayed
at 100 through t = 1, where the exact mean is 1.75. Where a linear table
ramps, stepTo() now places the next firing where the propensity
integrated from the current time reaches an Exp(1) draw, the
time-change theorem, and picks the reaction from the propensities read
there. The integral is taken over error-controlled 5-point Gauss-Lobatto
panels, the scheme bngsim's network SSA and RuleMonkey use, each node a
propensity refresh with the clock moved there. No panel spans a
breakpoint, and the last node before one is read an ulp short of it, so
a step table that jumps there is read on the side being integrated. A
call that stops inside a ramp leaves the rest of its draw to the next
call; a breakpoint drops it, as it drops a waiting time. Outside every
linear table's ramp the propensities are constant between firings and
stepTo() runs as before.
